<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Weight filtration on the cohomology
<br class="ltx_break">of algebraic varieties</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Masaki Hanamura
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Mathematical Institute, Tohoku University, Sendai 980 JAPAN
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:hanamura@math.tohoku.ac.jp">hanamura@math.tohoku.ac.jp</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Morihiko Saito
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>RIMS Kyoto University, Kyoto 606-8502 Japan
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:msaito@kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp">msaito@kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: June 15, 2006, v.2</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that the etale cohomology (with compact supports) of an
algebraic variety <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over an algebraically closed field has
the canonical weight filtration <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>,
and prove that the middle weight part of the cohomology with
compact supports of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a subspace of the intersection cohomology
of a compactification <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of X, or equivalently, the middle weight
part of the (so-called) Borel-Moore homology of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient of
the intersection cohomology of <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We are informed that this has been shown by A. Weber in the case
<math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is proper (and <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>)
using a theorem of G. Barthel, J.-P. Brasselet, K.-H. Fieseler,
O. Gabber and L. Kaup on morphisms between intersection
complexes.
We show that the assertion immediately follows from Gabber’s purity
theorem for intersection complexes.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Introduction</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible algebraic variety defined over an
algebraically closed field
<math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let
<math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denote (so-called) Borel-Moore homology,
i.e. the dual of
<math id="p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here cohomology means etale cohomology with
<math id="p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-adic coefficients where
<math id="p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime number different from
<math id="p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k</annotation></semantics></math> (and similarly for cohomology with
compact supports).
In the case
<math id="p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may also assume that it is singular cohomology with
<math id="p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients as in [1]
using the comparison theorem.
If
<math id="p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the algebraic closure of a finite field, it is known
that these cohomology and homology have the canonical weight
filtration
<math id="p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> so that
<math id="p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> has weights
<math id="p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq-j</annotation></semantics></math>,
see [2], [5], [7].
However, the general case of characteristic <math id="p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> does not seem
to be treated in the references.
In this paper, we show that this easily follows from [2], [6]
using a model, see (1.2).</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a compactification of
<math id="p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math> denote intersection cohomology, see [2], [10].
Note that
<math id="p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight
<math id="p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> by Gabber’s purity theorem [2] together with the stability of
pure complexes by the direct image under a proper morphism [7].
We have a canonical morphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(\overline{X})(n)\to H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(\overline{X})(n)\to H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is the dual of
<math id="p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X)\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X)\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Tate twist.
It was shown by G. Barthel, J.-P. Brasselet, K.-H. Fieseler, O. Gabber
and L. Kaup (see [1]) that any algebraic cycle classes can be lifted
(noncanonically) to the intersection cohomology by the above morphism
at least when
<math id="p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this paper we prove</p>
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.</span> <span id="p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The canonical morphism
<math id="p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(\overline{X})(n)\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{-j}H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(\overline{X})(n)\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{-j}H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, or
equivalently,
<math id="p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{j}H_{c}^{j}(X)\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{j}H_{c}^{j}(X)\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p5.1" class="ltx_p">This is a refinement of the theorem in [1] explained above, since the
cycle class of an algebraic
<math id="p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle is defined in
<math id="p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{-2d}H_{2d}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X))(-d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{-2d}H_{2d}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X))(-d)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We are informed that the assertion has been proved by A. Weber [22]
in the case <math id="p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is proper (and <math id="p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>)
using a theorem in [1] on morphisms between intersection complexes.
In the case
<math id="p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem 1 follows from the construction of
mixed Hodge structure in [5].
In general we have the surjectivity of the canonical morphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(\overline{X})(n)\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{-j}^{W}(\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(X)(n))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(\overline{X})(n)\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{-j}^{W}(\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(X)(n)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p5.2" class="ltx_p">factoring the first morphism in Theorem 1,
see Remark (i) in (2.2).
The proof of Theorem 1 immediately follows from Gabber’s purity theorem
for intersection complexes ([2], [3]).
We also give a shorter proof of the theorem in [1] on morphisms
between intersection complexes (here it is not necessary to assume
<math id="p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
</div>
<div id="p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span> (see [1]).
<span id="p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let
<math id="p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of equidimensional varieties over
an algebraically closed field
<math id="p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a noncanonical morphism</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whose composition with the canonical morphism
<math id="p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the
canonical morphism
<math id="p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p7.1" class="ltx_p">In [1], this was proved by reducing to the case where
<math id="p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subvariety of codimension
<math id="p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and using induction on stratum, see also [21].
We show that Theorem 2 immediately follows from Gabber’s purity
Theorem.
In the case of a closed embedding of irreducible varieties with relative
dimension
<math id="p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, they also showed the existence of a canonical morphism,
see [1].
We give another proof of it using the weight filtration on mixed perverse
sheaves [2], see (2.4).</p>
</div>
<div id="p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p8.1" class="ltx_p">If
<math id="p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible,
<math id="p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper surjective, and
<math id="p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a shifted perverse sheaf on
<math id="p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. if
<math id="p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=\dim Y=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=\dim Y=2</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and surjective), then the semisimplicity of pure
perverse sheaves [2] implies the canonical morphisms</p>
<table id="S0.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p8.2" class="ltx_p">This can be used to construct cohomological correspondences, see [8].</p>
</div>
<div id="p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p9.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is a consequence of our discussions on the first author’s
work [14] (see also [4]) at the conferences at Kagoshima (December 2004)
and at Baltimore (March 2005).
We thank the organizers of the conferences.
We also thank H. Esnault and L. Illusie for useful comments and questions
about the canonical choice of the morphisms, and
O. Gabber for pointing out an error in an early version of this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p10.1" class="ltx_p">In Section 1 we show the existence of the weight filtration on the cohomology
of a variety over any algebraically closed field.
In Section 2 we prove the main theorems using Gabber’s purity theorem.
In Section 3 we give an application of the decomposition theorem
of Beilinson, Bernstein and Deligne.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p11.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Preliminaries</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.1. Intersection cohomology.</span>
Let
<math id="p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an algebraic variety (i.e. a separated reduced scheme of
finite type) over an algebraically closed field.
Let
<math id="p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime different from the characteristic of
<math id="p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
By [2], [7], we have the bounded derived category with constructible
cohomology
<math id="p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the category of perverse sheaves
<math id="p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Perv}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Perv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Perv}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be a full subcategory of
<math id="p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying certain conditions, and
it is an abelian category, see [2].</p>
</div>
<div id="p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p13.1" class="ltx_p">If
<math id="p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is purely
<math id="p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, there is uniquely a shifted perverse sheaf
<math id="p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\in D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\in D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})[n]\in\hbox{\rm Perv}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>Perv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})[n]\in\hbox{\rm Perv}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of
<math id="p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> to the smooth part
<math id="p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the constant sheaf
<math id="p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})[n]</annotation></semantics></math> has no nontrivial sub nor quotient objects in
<math id="p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Perv}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Perv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Perv}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> which are supported on the complement of
<math id="p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, see [2].
This is called the intersection complex.
To simplify the notation, we use the above normalization of
intersection complex as in [1], which is different from [2].
We have unique morphisms</p>
<table id="S0.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.1.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(n)[2n]\to{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\quad\text{on}\quad X," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mtext>on</mtext><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(n)[2n]\to{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\quad\text{on}\quad X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p13.2" class="ltx_p">which are dual of each other, and
whose restriction to the smooth part
<math id="p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity on
<math id="p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here
<math id="p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Tate twist,
<math id="p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant sheaf on
<math id="p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of
<math id="p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> which is isomorphic to the dualizing complex
<math id="p13.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:X\to\hbox{\rm Spec}\,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext>Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:X\to\hbox{\rm Spec}\,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first morphism of (1.1.1) follows from the fact that
<math id="p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{j}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{j}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})[n]</annotation></semantics></math> is a canonical quotient of
<math id="p13.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the last assertion, note that
<math id="p13.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> has no nontrivial quotient
<math id="p13.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> whose support has dimension
<math id="p13.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="p13.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the perverse cohomology sheaf.
(Indeed, this follows from</p>
<table id="S0.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Hom}({}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},M)=\hbox{\rm Hom}({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],M)=\hbox{\rm Hom}(i^{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],i^{*}M)=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Hom}({}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},M)=\hbox{\rm Hom}({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],M)=\hbox{\rm Hom}(i^{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],i^{*}M)=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p13.3" class="ltx_p">by the vanishing of negative extension groups for perverse sheaves [2],
where
<math id="p13.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the inclusion of the support of
<math id="p13.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="p13.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p14.1" class="ltx_p">We define intersection cohomology (with
compact supports) by</p>
<table id="S0.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=H^{j}(X,\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),\quad\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=H_{c}^{j}(X,\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=H^{j}(X,\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),\quad\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=H_{c}^{j}(X,\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p14.2" class="ltx_p">We will denote their dual by
<math id="p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{\text{\rm BM}}_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{\text{\rm BM}}_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
These are isomorphic to
<math id="p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{2n-j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{2n-j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(n)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p15.1" class="ltx_p">By (1.1.1) we have canonical morphisms</p>
<table id="S0.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.1.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),\quad\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(n)\to H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),\quad\hbox{\rm IH}^{2n-j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(n)\to H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p15.2" class="ltx_p">which are dual of each other.
Here
<math id="p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}):=H^{-j}(X,{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}):=H^{-j}(X,{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes (so-called)
Borel-Moore homology which is the dual of
<math id="p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.2. Weight filtration.</span>
If
<math id="p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic closure of a finite field, then there is the
weight filtration
<math id="p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and hence on their dual
<math id="p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover,
<math id="p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> have weights
<math id="p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq-j</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, i.e.
<math id="p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Gr}_{i}^{W}H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=\hbox{\rm Gr}_{-i}^{W}H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Gr}_{i}^{W}H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=\hbox{\rm Gr}_{-i}^{W}H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
for
<math id="p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, see [2], [7].</p>
</div>
<div id="p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p17.1" class="ltx_p">It is known that these can be generalized to the case of any
algebraically closed field.
Indeed, if
<math id="p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> has characteristic
<math id="p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>,
then this is well known as part of the theory of mixed
motives via realizations, see [17].
The argument is similar for
<math id="p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, we may assume that
<math id="p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over a subfield
<math id="p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> which is finitely generated over
<math id="p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathbb{F}}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathbb{F}}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathbb{F}}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathbb{F}}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the algebraic closure of a finite field
<math id="p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a morphism
<math id="p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> of algebraic varieties over
<math id="p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathbb{F}}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathbb{F}}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the function field of
<math id="p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the geometric generic fiber of
<math id="p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> over the geometric generic point of
<math id="p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the inclusion
<math id="p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}\to k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}\to k</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with
<math id="p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we may assume that
<math id="p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{j}\pi_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{j}\pi_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p17.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{j}\pi_{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{j}\pi_{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth sheaves
shrinking
<math id="p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p18.1" class="ltx_p">By [2] these smooth sheaves have the canonical weight filtration
<math id="p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, which is compatible with the weight filtration on
<math id="p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}({\mathcal{X}}_{s},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}({\mathcal{X}}_{s},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}({\mathcal{X}}_{s},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}({\mathcal{X}}_{s},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> for any closed point
<math id="p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> using the generic base change theorem [6].
Indeed, the stalk at <math id="p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> of the smooth pure perverse sheaf <math id="p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> of
weight <math id="p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight <math id="p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-d</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\dim S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\dim S</annotation></semantics></math>, because
<math id="p18.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}^{!}{\mathcal{F}}=i_{s}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}\otimes i_{s}^{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}^{!}{\mathcal{F}}=i_{s}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}\otimes i_{s}^{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for any smooth <math id="p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}:\hbox{\rm Spec}\,k\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}:\hbox{\rm Spec}\,k\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
Restricting to the stalk at the geometric generic point,
this induces the weight filtration
<math id="p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, and then on
<math id="p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}^{\text{\rm BM}}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> by duality.
The obtained filtration is independent of the choice of
<math id="p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime},S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime},S</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is functorial for morphisms of algebraic varieties in the usual
way,
because any morphism over <math id="p18.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> has a model over <math id="p18.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> replacing <math id="p18.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> if
necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.3. Remark.</span>
Let
<math id="p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathcal{X}}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be as above.
Then the pull-back of the intersection complex
<math id="p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{{\mathcal{X}}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{{\mathcal{X}}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> by the canonical morphism
<math id="p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally isomorphic to
<math id="p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> up to a shift of complexes.
This can be shown by using the intermediate direct image [2]
together with the generic base change theorem [6].
As a corollary we get the purity of intersection cohomology in
the proper case using the above construction of weight filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p20.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Proof of the main theorems</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.1. Proof of Theorem 1.</span>
Note that the canonical morphisms in Theorem 1 is
induced by (1.1.2) together with the restriction morphism
(and its dual).
We first reduce the assertion to the case where
<math id="p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic closure of a finite field
<math id="p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the case
<math id="p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k=p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k=p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
this follows from Remark (1.3) taking a closed point of
<math id="p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, because the canonical morphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{j}H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{j}H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p21.2" class="ltx_p">can be extended to a morphism of local systems</p>
<table id="S0.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{j}R^{j}\pi_{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to R^{j}\overline{\pi}_{!}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>!</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Gr}_{W}^{j}R^{j}\pi_{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to R^{j}\overline{\pi}_{!}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p21.3" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\pi}:\overline{\mathcal{X}}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\pi}:\overline{\mathcal{X}}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is a compactification of
<math id="p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> whose geometric generic fiber is
<math id="p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If
<math id="p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
the argument is similar using the mod
<math id="p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> reduction argument in [2].
So we may assume that
<math id="p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the algebraic closure of a finite field.</p>
</div>
<div id="p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mapping cone
<math id="p22.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C(j^{\prime}_{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(j^{\prime}_{!}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}:X\to\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}:X\to\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the inclusion morphism.
By the associated long exact sequence</p>
<table id="S0.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to H^{j-1}(\overline{X},K)\to H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to H^{j-1}(\overline{X},K)\to H_{c}^{j}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X},{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p22.2" class="ltx_p">it is sufficient to show that
<math id="p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j-1}(\overline{X},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j-1}(\overline{X},K)</annotation></semantics></math> has weights
<math id="p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq j-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
By [2], 5.1.14, this is reduced to the assertion that
<math id="p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> has weights
<math id="p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> in the sense of [2], 5.1.8.
By definition this is equivalent to the condition that
<math id="p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}^{j}K_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}^{j}K_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> has weights
<math id="p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> for any closed point
<math id="p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
But this is further equivalent to the same assertion with
<math id="p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by
<math id="p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, because
<math id="p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}^{j}K_{x}={\mathcal{H}}^{j}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x}/{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}^{j}K_{x}={\mathcal{H}}^{j}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x}/{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}^{j}K_{x}={\mathcal{H}}^{j}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}^{j}K_{x}={\mathcal{H}}^{j}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
(The isomorphism for
<math id="p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the fact that the constant sheaf
<math id="p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> has no nontrivial subsheaf supported on a closed
subvariety of strictly smaller dimension, which we apply to the
kernel of
<math id="p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of the first morphism in (1.1.1).)
Then the above assertion for the intersection complex
<math id="p22.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is known as
Gabber’s purity theorem [2].
This completes the proof of Theorem 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="p23" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.2. Remarks.</span>
(i) The canonical morphism
<math id="p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{j}^{W}\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(\overline{X})\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{j}^{W}\hbox{\rm IH}^{j}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective.
This follows from the distinguished triangle</p>
<table id="S0.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{*}i^{!}\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbf{R}}j^{\prime}_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\to}\limits^{+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}i^{!}\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbf{R}}j^{\prime}_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\to}\limits^{+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p23.2" class="ltx_p">(where
<math id="p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:\overline{X}\setminus X\to\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:\overline{X}\setminus X\to\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inclusion of the complement)
together with the assertion that
<math id="p23.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i^{!}\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{!}\hbox{\rm IC}_{\overline{X}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> has weights
<math id="p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, see [2], 5.1.14.</p>
</div>
<div id="p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p24.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) If
<math id="p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and
<math id="p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have a canonical choice of a splitting of
the surjective or injective morphisms in Theorem 1 using a polarization
of Hodge structure.
However, it is not clear whether it is a good one.
For example, certain algebraic cycle classes (i.e. the graph of
‘placid’ maps) can be canonically lifted to the intersection cohomology
in [11] (see also [1]), and the relation with this is unclear.
This problem of canonical lifting is related to the Lefschetz
trace formula for intersection cohomology
(see [9], [11], [12], [13], [15], [16], [18], [19], [20], [23]),
and will be treated in [8].</p>
</div>
<div id="p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.3. Proof of Theorem 2.</span>
Let
<math id="p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=C({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=C({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the distinguished triangle</p>
<table id="S0.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.3.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}K[-1]\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\to}\limits^{+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}K[-1]\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\to}\limits^{+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p25.2" class="ltx_p">the assertion is
equivalent to the vanishing of the composition of canonical morphisms</p>
<table id="S0.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.3.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}K[-1]\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\quad\hbox{in}\,\,\,D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}K[-1]\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\quad\hbox{in}\,\,\,D_{c}^{b}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p25.3" class="ltx_p">Then the assertion is reduced to the case where
<math id="p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic closure of a finite field.
Indeed, in the positive characteristic case, we have</p>
<table id="S0.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Hom}(f^{*}K[-1],\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=H^{1}(X,{\mathbf{R}}{\mathcal{H}}{om}(f^{*}K,\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Hom}(f^{*}K[-1],\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})=H^{1}(X,{\mathbf{R}}{\mathcal{H}}{om}(f^{*}K,\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p25.4" class="ltx_p">and this vector space can be extended to a
local system (i.e. a smooth sheaf) on
<math id="p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as in (1.2).
Note that (2.3.2) is extended to a section of this local system and
its vanishing is equivalent to that for its restriction
over a closed point using the generic base change theorem [6].
(If
<math id="p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, it is also possible to replace
<math id="p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (1.3).)
In the case
<math id="p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this follows from the mod
<math id="p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> reduction argument in [2].</p>
</div>
<div id="p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p26.1" class="ltx_p">By the same argument as in (2.1),
<math id="p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}K</annotation></semantics></math> has weights
<math id="p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a finite subfield
<math id="p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> together with a morphism
<math id="p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}:X_{0}\to Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}:X_{0}\to Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of algebraic varieties over
<math id="p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the base change of
<math id="p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the above morphisms are defined over
<math id="p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the assertion follows from [2], 5.1.15 (applied to
<math id="p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}K</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> which have weights
<math id="p26.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p26.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> respectively with the normalization of intersection
complexes in [1]).
This completes the proof of Theorem 2.</p>
</div>
<div id="p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p27.1" class="ltx_p">The following was proved in [1] using induction on stratum at least if
<math id="p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>char</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm char}\,k=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We give another proof using the weight filtration
<math id="p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on mixed perverse sheaves [2].</p>
</div>
<div id="p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.4. Proposition.</span>
<span id="p28.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume
<math id="p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible,
<math id="p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed immersion, and
<math id="p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y=\dim X+1\,(=n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.559em;" width="0.559em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y=\dim X+1\,(=n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a canonical choice of the morphism
<math id="p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the
condition in Theorem 2.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p29.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.</span>
Let
<math id="p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:Y\setminus X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:Y\setminus X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inclusion morphism.
We have the long exact sequence</p>
<table id="S0.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\to{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f_{*}{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i+1}j_{!}j^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f_{*}{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i+1}j_{!}j^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p29.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="p29.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i+1}j_{!}j^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>IC</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i+1}j_{!}j^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1&gt;n+1\,(=\dim Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.559em;" width="0.559em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1&gt;n+1\,(=\dim Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, see [2], 4.2.4.
So we get</p>
<table id="S0.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=0\quad\text{for}\,\,\,i&gt;n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{i}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=0\quad\text{for}\,\,\,i&gt;n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p29.3" class="ltx_p">because the perverse sheaf
<math id="p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n+1]</annotation></semantics></math> has no nontrivial quotient whose support
is contained in
<math id="p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore,
<math id="p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence
<math id="p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> (see [2], 5.4.1) have weights
<math id="p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus we get a canonical morphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{n}^{W}{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to\hbox{\rm Gr}_{n}^{W}{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p29.4" class="ltx_p">(factoring through
<math id="p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}{\mathcal{H}}^{n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>).
By the semisimplicity theorem ([2], 5.3.8),
the target of the above morphism is semisimple, and is a direct sum of
a subobject whose support is strictly smaller than
<math id="p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and an intersection complex with support
<math id="p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> associated with a smooth sheaf defined on a dense open subvariety of
<math id="p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
So a morphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.4.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p29.5" class="ltx_p">is uniquely determined by its restriction to any sufficiently small
nonempty open subvariety
<math id="p29.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p29.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (contained in the smooth part of
<math id="p29.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>), because
<math id="p29.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> is a perverse sheaf and is pure of weight
<math id="p29.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have a similar assertion for a morphism
<math id="p29.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p30.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime};X^{\prime}\to Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime};X^{\prime}\to Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the restriction (or base change) of
<math id="p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> over an open subvariety
<math id="p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> whose complement has codimension
<math id="p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let
<math id="p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\prime}:\widetilde{Y}^{\prime}\to Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\prime}:\widetilde{Y}^{\prime}\to Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normalization, and
<math id="p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}=(\widetilde{Y}^{\prime}\times_{Y^{\prime}}X^{\prime})_{\text{\rm red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>red</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}=(\widetilde{Y}^{\prime}\times_{Y^{\prime}}X^{\prime})_{\text{\rm red}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the canonical
morphism
<math id="p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\prime\prime}:X^{\prime\prime}\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\prime\prime}:X^{\prime\prime}\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let
<math id="p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the irreducible components of
<math id="p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the separable degree of the extension
<math id="p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(X^{\prime\prime}_{i})/k(X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(X^{\prime\prime}_{i})/k(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we may assume that
<math id="p30.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Y}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Y}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p30.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth over
<math id="p30.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p30.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{\prime}{}^{*}\rho^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}{}^{*}\rho^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a local system
(shrinking
<math id="p30.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary).
Then we have</p>
<table id="S0.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y^{\prime}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\rho^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y^{\prime}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y^{\prime}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\rho^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y^{\prime}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p30.2" class="ltx_p">and the restriction of the morphism (2.4.1) to
<math id="p30.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by
<math id="p30.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p31.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the irreducible variety with finite morphisms
<math id="p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}_{i}\to X_{i}^{s}\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}_{i}\to X_{i}^{s}\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factoring
<math id="p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the function field
<math id="p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(X_{i}^{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(X_{i}^{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximal separable extension of
<math id="p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> contained in
<math id="p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(X^{\prime\prime}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(X^{\prime\prime}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> (shrinking
<math id="p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary).
Then the direct image of the constant sheaf
<math id="p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}_{i}\to X_{i}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}_{i}\to X_{i}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the constant sheaf
<math id="p31.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> so that we may replace
<math id="p31.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\prime\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="p31.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="p31.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the canonical morphism of the disjoint union of the
<math id="p31.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="p31.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So the desired morphism
<math id="p31.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the dual of the
canonical morphism
<math id="p31.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, divided by
<math id="p31.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, because the composition of
<math id="p31.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> and its dual
<math id="p31.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\vee}:\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\vee}:\rho^{s}_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
multiplication by
<math id="p31.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="p31.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This completes the proof of Proposition (2.4).</p>
</div>
<div id="p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.5. Remarks.</span> (i)
Even in the case of Proposition (2.4), the morphism satisfying the
condition in Theorem 2 is not unique in general (for example, if
<math id="p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has etale locally two components whose intersection is
<math id="p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>).
However, it is unique if
<math id="p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is etale locally irreducible at the generic point of
<math id="p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, see also [1].</p>
</div>
<div id="p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p33.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) In the case
<math id="p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed immersion of codimension
<math id="p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism satisfying the condition in Theorem 2 is not
unique even if
<math id="p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> have only isolated singularities.
For simplicity, assume
<math id="p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y=\dim X+2\,(=n+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.559em;" width="0.559em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y=\dim X+2\,(=n+2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For
<math id="p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\hbox{\rm Sing}\,X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>Sing</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\hbox{\rm Sing}\,X</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S0.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{X,x}^{i}={\mathcal{H}}^{i}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x},\quad E_{Y,x}^{i}={\mathcal{H}}^{i}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{X,x}^{i}={\mathcal{H}}^{i}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x},\quad E_{Y,x}^{i}={\mathcal{H}}^{i}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})_{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p33.2" class="ltx_p">Then we have a distinguished triangle</p>
<table id="S0.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}\tau_{\leq n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to E_{Y,x}^{n+1}[-n-1]\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\longrightarrow}\limits^{+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>τ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}\tau_{\leq n}f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to f^{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to E_{Y,x}^{n+1}[-n-1]\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\longrightarrow}\limits^{+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p33.3" class="ltx_p">and there is a contribution of</p>
<table id="S0.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Hom}(E_{Y,x}^{n+1}[-n-1],\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Hom}(E_{Y,x}^{n+1}[-n-1],\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p33.4" class="ltx_p">to the
ambiguity of the morphism using the above distinguished triangle
together with the vanishing of negative extensions.
Moreover, by the self duality</p>
<table id="S0.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(n)[2n]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}=\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(n)[2n],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p33.5" class="ltx_p">the last group is isomorphic to</p>
<table id="S0.Ex25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S0.Ex25X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex25X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Hom}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},({\mathbb{D}}E_{Y,x}^{n+1})(-n)[-n+1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Hom}(\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},({\mathbb{D}}E_{Y,x}^{n+1})(-n)[-n+1])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S0.Ex25Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex25Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Hom}(E_{X,x}^{n-1},({\mathbb{D}}E_{Y,x}^{n+1})(-n))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.278em">=</mo><mrow><mtext>Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Hom}(E_{X,x}^{n-1},({\mathbb{D}}E_{Y,x}^{n+1})(-n)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p33.6" class="ltx_p">which is not necessarily zero in general.
For example, if
<math id="p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the affine
cone of a smooth projective variety
<math id="p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{X,x}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{X,x}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the primitive cohomology
<math id="p33.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}_{\text{\rm prim}}(V,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mtext>prim</mtext><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}_{\text{\rm prim}}(V,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p34.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p34.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3. Application of the decomposition theorem</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.1. Decomposition theorem.</span>
Let <math id="p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper surjective morphism of irreducible algebraic
varieties over an algebraically closed field <math id="p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the decomposition theorem [2], we have a noncanonical isomorphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.1.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\simeq\hbox{$\bigoplus$}_{i,Z}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]\quad\text{in}\quad D^{b}_{c}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>in</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\simeq\hbox{$\bigoplus$}_{i,Z}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]\quad\text{in}\quad D^{b}_{c}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p35.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible reduced closed subvarieties of <math id="p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{i}_{Z^{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{i}_{Z^{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth <math id="p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-adic sheaves defined on dense open
smooth subvarieties
<math id="p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> which we may assume to be independent of <math id="p35.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
This can be reduced to the case where the base field
<math id="p35.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is finite by using a model as in (1.2) together with [6].
Note that the shift by <math id="p35.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="p35.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math> is needed for
<math id="p35.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="p35.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> because of the normalization
of the intersection complex in this paper.
Set</p>
<table id="S0.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{Z}=\max\{i\,|\,E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}\neq 0\}=\max\{i\,|\,E_{Z^{\circ}}^{-i}\neq 0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{Z}=\max\{i\,|\,E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}\neq 0\}=\max\{i\,|\,E_{Z^{\circ}}^{-i}\neq 0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p35.3" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="p35.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of <math id="p35.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{Z^{\circ}}^{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{Z^{\circ}}^{-i}</annotation></semantics></math>
up to a Tate twist.</p>
</div>
<div id="p36" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p36.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.2. Lemma.</span> <span id="p36.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With the above notation, assume <math id="p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> so that
<math id="p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}={\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}={\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.2.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{Z}\leq n-\dim Z-2\,\,\,\hbox{if}\,\,\,Z\neq Y,\quad m_{Y}\leq n-\dim Y." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{Z}\leq n-\dim Z-2\,\,\,\hbox{if}\,\,\,Z\neq Y,\quad m_{Y}\leq n-\dim Y.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="p37" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p37.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}=f^{-1}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}=f^{-1}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a general closed point <math id="p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S0.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{Z}\leq 2\dim X_{s}+\dim Z-n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{Z}\leq 2\dim X_{s}+\dim Z-n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p37.2" class="ltx_p">because the stalk of <math id="p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{j}f_{*}({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{j}f_{*}({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n])</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>
vanishes unless <math id="p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[-n,2\dim X_{s}-n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[-n,2\dim X_{s}-n]</annotation></semantics></math> and the stalk of
<math id="p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{i}_{Z^{\circ},s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{i}_{Z^{\circ},s}</annotation></semantics></math> put at
the degree <math id="p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=i-\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=i-\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have <math id="p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X_{s}\leq n-\dim Z-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X_{s}\leq n-\dim Z-1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\neq Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\neq Y</annotation></semantics></math>. So the assertion follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p38.1" class="ltx_p">As a corollary we have the following
(which is used in [8]).</p>
</div>
<div id="p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.3. Proposition.</span> <span id="p39.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With the notation of <math id="p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3.1)</annotation></semantics></math>, assume <math id="p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> smooth.
Let</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],\quad v:{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(-n)[-n]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],\quad v:{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(-n)[-n]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p39.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="p39.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the canonical morphisms on <math id="p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of <math id="p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S0.Ex31X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.3.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex31X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{Z,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{Z,i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex31X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S0.Ex31Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex31Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{Z,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{Z,i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex31Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]\to{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(-n)[-n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i]\to{\mathbb{D}}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(-n)[-n]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p39.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="p39.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the induced morphisms using the decomposition <math id="p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3.1.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.1.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3.1.1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then
</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S0.Ex32X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex32.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.3.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex32X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{Z,i}=0\,\,\,\text{for}\,\,\,(Z,i)\neq(Y,n-\dim Y)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{Z,i}=0\,\,\,\text{for}\,\,\,(Z,i)\neq(Y,n-\dim Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S0.Ex32Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex32Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{Z,i}=0\,\,\,\text{for}\,\,\,(Z,i)\neq(Y,\dim Y-n)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{Z,i}=0\,\,\,\text{for}\,\,\,(Z,i)\neq(Y,\dim Y-n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="p40" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p40.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.</span>
By the adjunction for the inclusion <math id="p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the canonical
isomorphism</p>
<table id="S0.Ex33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S0.Ex33X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex33X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Hom}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Hom</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Hom}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex33X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n],\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][-i])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S0.Ex33Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex33Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Hom}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>Hom</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Hom}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex33Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[\dim Z],\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][\dim Z-n-i])," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle({\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[\dim Z],\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{\circ}}^{i}[\dim Z][\dim Z-n-i]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p40.2" class="ltx_p">and the last group vanishes for <math id="p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z-n-i&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z-n-i&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
by the semi-perversity of the constant sheaf <math id="p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[\dim Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[\dim Z]</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
So we get the first assertion by (3.2.1).
The assertion is similar for the second.</p>
</div>
<div id="p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p41.1" class="ltx_p">As a corollary of (3.3), we have</p>
</div>
<div id="p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p42.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.4. Corollary.</span> <span id="p42.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper surjective morphism of irreducible algebraic
varieties over an algebraically closed field <math id="p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume <math id="p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then
</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S0.Ex34X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Ker}(H^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H^{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Ker}(H^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H^{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Ker}(H^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Ker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Ker}(H^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S0.Ex34Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Ker}(H_{c}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H_{c}^{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Ker}(H_{c}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H_{c}^{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Ker}(H_{c}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Ker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Ker}(H_{c}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to\hbox{\rm IH}_{c}^{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S0.Ex34Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Im}(H_{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Im}(H_{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Im}(\hbox{\rm IH}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Im}(\hbox{\rm IH}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S0.Ex34Xc"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Im}(H^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hbox{\rm Im}(H^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S0.Ex34Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Im}(\hbox{\rm IH}^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>BM</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hbox{\rm Im}(\hbox{\rm IH}^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\to H^{\text{\rm BM}}_{i}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="p43" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p43.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.</span>
This follows from (3.3).</p>
</div>
<div id="p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.5. Remarks.</span>
(i) The above corollary is related to a question of A. Weber
when <math id="p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a resolution of singularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p45.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) The first isomorphism of (3.4) implies a proof of Theorem 1 in the
case <math id="p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> proper, using the weight spectral sequence in [5].
Indeed, if <math id="p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a resolution of singularities, then
<math id="p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Gr}^{W}_{i}H^{i}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Gr}^{W}_{i}H^{i}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is a subspace of <math id="p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (which coincides with the image
of <math id="p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>) by the weight spectral sequence associated to a simplicial
resolution as in Remark (iii) below.
So we can replace <math id="p45.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="p45.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{i}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{i}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> by (3.4).</p>
</div>
<div id="p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p46.1" class="ltx_p">(iii)
Let <math id="p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper irreducible variety of dimension <math id="p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> over an
algebraically closed field <math id="p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the natural morphisms</p>
<table id="S0.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm Gr}_{n}^{W}H^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\longrightarrow}\limits^{u_{n}}}\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\longrightarrow}\limits^{v_{n}}}\hbox{\rm Gr}_{n}^{W}(H_{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(-n))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Gr</mtext><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm Gr}_{n}^{W}H^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\longrightarrow}\limits^{u_{n}}}\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\longrightarrow}\limits^{v_{n}}}\hbox{\rm Gr}_{n}^{W}(H_{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(-n)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p46.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective and <math id="p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of <math id="p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
and is surjective.
These are induced by the morphisms
<math id="p46.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> in (3.3).
We have a canonical self-pairing of <math id="p46.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and a canonical pairing between <math id="p46.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="p46.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})(-n)</annotation></semantics></math> with values in <math id="p46.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}(-n)</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
<table id="S0.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex36.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.5.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle u(\xi),\eta\rangle=\langle\xi,v(\eta)\rangle\quad\text{for}\,\,\,\xi\in H^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),v\in\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle u(\xi),\eta\rangle=\langle\xi,v(\eta)\rangle\quad\text{for}\,\,\,\xi\in H^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}),v\in\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p46.3" class="ltx_p">By (3.3) and (3.4) we can replace
<math id="p46.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="p46.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="p46.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a resolution of singularities of <math id="p46.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
By (3.1.1) <math id="p46.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>IH</mtext><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IH}^{n}(Y,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct factor of <math id="p46.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,{\mathbb{Q}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>
noncanonically,
and the morphisms <math id="p46.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p46.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible with
any decomposition (3.1.1) by (3.3).</p>
</div>
<div id="p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p47.1" class="ltx_p">(iv) With the above notation, the restriction of the self-pairing to
the image of <math id="p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> does not seem to be nondegenerate in general.
Indeed, if we take a simplicial resolution <math id="p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\raise 0.60275pt\hbox{${\scriptscriptstyle\bullet}$}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mpadded voffset="0.6pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em">∙</mo></mpadded></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\raise 0.60275pt\hbox{${\scriptscriptstyle\bullet}$}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=X</annotation></semantics></math> in the case of <math id="p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then this image coincides with the kernel of <math id="p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X)\to H^{n}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X)\to H^{n}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, this does not seem to be compatible with the Lefschetz
decomposition in general.</p>
</div>
<div id="p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p48.1" class="ltx_p">For example, let <math id="p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the blow-up of <math id="p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> along a point.
This is a <math id="p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C={\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C={\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> having disjoint two sections
<math id="p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> whose self-intersection number is <math id="p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there is a variety <math id="p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> which is obtained by identifying the
two sections <math id="p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the restriction of the canonical
pairing to the kernel of <math id="p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(X)\to H^{2}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(X)\to H^{2}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> would be degenerate,
where the last morphism is defined by the difference between the
restriction morphisms to <math id="p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (both identified with <math id="p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
naturally).
However, it is not clear if such <math id="p48.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> exists in the category of algebraic
varieties (although it exists as an analytic space if <math id="p48.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k={\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k={\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
It would not be projective at least.</p>
</div>
<div id="p49" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p49.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p49.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.6. Remark.</span>
Let
<math id="p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper surjective morphism of irreducible
varieties over an algebraically closed field <math id="p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> as in (3.1).
Let <math id="p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume</p>
<table id="S0.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex37.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.6.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\,\,\text{is a perverse sheaf on}\,\,Y." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>is a perverse sheaf on</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]\,\,\text{is a perverse sheaf on}\,\,Y.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p49.2" class="ltx_p">This assumption implies that <math id="p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=\dim Y=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=\dim Y=n</annotation></semantics></math>, and the direct sum
decomposition (3.1.1) becomes the canonical decomposition in the category
of perverse sheaves</p>
<table id="S0.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex38.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.6.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]=\hbox{$\bigoplus$}_{Z}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{o}}[\dim Z]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>Z</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}[n]=\hbox{$\bigoplus$}_{Z}\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{o}}[\dim Z],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p49.3" class="ltx_p">because there are no nontrivial morphisms between intersection
complexes with different supports.
This induces canonical morphisms</p>
<table id="S0.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l},\quad{\mathbf{R}}f_{*}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}\to\hbox{\rm IC}_{Y}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="p49.4" class="ltx_p">Indeed, over a sufficiently small non-empty open subvariety of
<math id="p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the intersection complexes coincide with the constant
sheaf
<math id="p49.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the assertion is clear.
Then we can extend the obtained morphisms uniquely over
<math id="p49.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> using the decomposition (3.6.2) together with the intermediate
direct image [2].
Here we can neglect
<math id="p49.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{\rm IC}_{Z}E_{Z^{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="p49.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\neq Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\neq Y</annotation></semantics></math>, because an intersection complex has no nontrivial sub nor
quotient objects with strictly smaller support.</p>
</div>
<div id="p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p50.1" class="ltx_p">The condition (3.6.1) is satisfied for example if
<math id="p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=\dim Y=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=\dim Y=2</annotation></semantics></math>, or
<math id="p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and surjective.
In the first case, (3.6.1) follows from the fact that
the support of
<math id="p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}^{1}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IC</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}^{1}\hbox{\rm IC}_{X}{\mathbb{Q}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is discrete.
In the second case, the direct image is an intersection complex,
because the direct image by a finite morphism is an exact functor
of perverse sheaves, see [2].</p>
</div>
